  {
   "provenance": "seed:gap_verify:observed-0",
   "src": {
    "type": "Agent",
    "var": "s"
   },
   "edge_type": "observed",
   "tgt": {
    "type": "Observation",
    "var": "t"
   }
  }
 ],
 "rules": [
  {
   "name": "gap_verify_intermediates",
   "comment": "CHECK THE ABDUCED INTERMEDIATES AGAINST WHAT WAS ACTUALLY OBSERVED. GapAbduce emits its intermediates (`bridged`) and compares them to NOTHING -- which is why it has NO DISCRIMINATIVE POWER: any two same-shape figures ARE rigid translates, so divisibility is its only constraint and it accepts independent random endpoints at ~1/steps. This rule supplies the missing constraint. On a gap-sequence Observation whose gap frames are OBSERVED rather than blank (the figure absent, the rest of the scene present), each abduced intermediate G^t(f_before) is a FALSIFIABLE PREDICTION about frame t, and it is CHECKED: every predicted cell must COINCIDE with some cell actually observed at the same integer coordinates. `vviolations` is the count that do not; `verified` iff that count is 0. There is NO TOLERANCE in this and nowhere to put one -- an in-frame comparison is integer by construction (a 'close enough' radius would be a CROSS-frame quantity smuggled in-frame), so PROTOCOL 13 is satisfied the strongest way available: no number at all. `vabduced` is the divisibility verdict ALONE, so the rule reports what the incumbent would have said next to what verification says -- a refusal is DIAGNOSABLE (`vreason`: ok/unverified/identity/indivisible/not_rigid/no_gap_structure/inconsistent_gap/hidden_mismatch/multi_motion) and a refusal-after-a-full-check (`vticks`,`vpredicted` non-zero) is distinguishable from a refusal at a guard. status flips to 'gap_verified'. WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: it does not infer occlusion ORDER, and on a genuinely BLANK gap nothing is observed, so every prediction is violated and classic phi is REFUSED -- this mechanism is MORE refusing than a human, by construction.",
   "match_type": "Observation",
   "match": [
    [
     "ag",
     "observed",
     "obs"
    ]
   ],
   "where": {
    "type": "And",
